# ---- Annotation-attribute enforcement (strict) -------------------
# Walks the AST of every TU under libs/, src/, examples/, tests/,
# port/ via libclang and applies the 19 ra8_* annotation rules
# documented in docs/ANNOTATIONS.md. The tree clears the gate, so it
# runs in --check (strict) mode here and in CI. When libclang is not
# installed locally the script exits 0 (graceful skip); CI installs a
# pinned libclang so the gate is enforced there unconditionally.
if [[ -f scripts/checks/check_annotations.py ]]; then
  if ! python3 scripts/checks/check_annotations.py --check --quiet; then
    echo "[FAIL] check_annotations.py reported issues."
    error_found=1
  fi
fi

# ---- Stack-usage aggregator (strict for first-party) --------------
# Re-runs the project-wide -fstack-usage aggregator over whatever
# .su files already exist under examples/*/*/build*/. We do NOT
# trigger a build here -- a fresh clone has no .su files yet and
# running `make stack-usage` from a hook would slow every commit by
# minutes. The script simply skips silently when nothing is built.
#
# Behaviour:
#   * Soft violations in *first-party* TUs (frame > 2048 bytes) --
#     HARD FAIL via --strict. Authors must either reduce the frame
#     (move scratch buffers to module-static storage) or enroll the
#     function in FIRST_PARTY_EXEMPTIONS at the top of
#     scripts/checks/stack_usage_check.py with a written rationale.
#   * Soft violations in *third-party* TUs (libs/third_party/) --
#     reported but ignored (SOUP, see docs/SOUP/).
#   * Critical-module breaches (ra8_isr/ra8_check/ra8_err/ra8_mpu/
#     ra8_cgc/ra8_pfs > 256 bytes) -- HARD FAIL via the existing
#     critical-path gate inside the script.
#   * `dynamic` qualifier anywhere -- HARD FAIL. NASA Power-of-10
#     Rule 3 forbids VLAs and alloca() in this firmware regardless
#     of frame size. No deviation procedure is offered.
#
# Promoted from warn-only to strict (first-party) on 2026-05-02 once
# every first-party function was verified at <2048 bytes against
# HEAD. See docs/STACK_USAGE.md.
if [[ -f scripts/checks/stack_usage_check.py ]]; then
  if ! python3 scripts/checks/stack_usage_check.py --strict --quiet; then
    echo "[FAIL] stack_usage_check.py: first-party frame > 2048 B"
    echo "       or NASA P10 dynamic-frame violation."
    error_found=1
  fi
fi

# ---- Clang Static Analyzer (scan-build) -- LOCAL ONLY, NOT GATED --
# scan-build is a multi-minute analyzer pass and is NOT invoked from
# the per-commit hook. Run it locally with `make scan-build` (or
# `make scan-build-strict`); see docs/STATIC_ANALYSIS.md for the
# documented baseline -- currently 0 actionable first-party findings
# once the HAL MMIO suppressions are applied.
#
# This comment used to promise that "regressions will be caught by
# CI's scan_build.sh --strict invocation". No workflow runs it, and
# RA8_GATE_REGISTRY in scripts/ci.sh has no scan-build gate, so that
# was a false assurance -- exactly as misleading as a gate that
# passes without checking anything. To make it true, add a gate to
# the registry and schedule it in a workflow; the ci-parity gate
# will hold the two halves together.

# ---- include-what-you-use (IWYU) -- warn-only --------------------
# Same as scan-build: too slow for the per-commit hook. Run locally
# with `make iwyu`. See scripts/checks/iwyu.sh.

